--TEST--
Spec § 18's `close` is the boundary every other `Core\Db\Connection` member is on the far side of: a statement, `driver()` and `serverVersion()` all throw `LogicError` afterwards, while `isOpen()` still answers and a second `close` does nothing — `rule:core-classes/db-statement-members` and `rule:security/db-pool-reset-is-a-boundary`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Db\Connection::close
// covers: Core\Db\Connection::driver
// The connection is real (SQLite), so the statement below is refused by the
// member and not by a signature check — the refusal is the one the running
// program would meet.
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");
var $made = $db->execute("create table t (id integer primary key)", []);
echo "created ", $made->affected(), "\n";

$db->close();

// A statement on a released connection is the program's mistake and not the
// network's, so it is a `LogicError` and never an `IOError`: nothing was
// attempted on a wire.
try {
    $db->query("select id from t", []);
    echo "query: not refused\n";
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    echo "query: refused\n";
}

// `stream` is on the same side, and it is the interesting one: § 4 makes it the
// member that *holds* the connection, so a program could reasonably expect it to
// be refused later — at the first row — rather than where the statement is
// written. It is not. Naming the walk is what opens the portal, so a released
// connection is found out at the call and never inside a `foreach`.
try {
    $db->stream("select id from t", []);
    echo "stream: not refused\n";
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    echo "stream: refused\n";
}

// `driver()` reaches for the connection too, so it is on the same side of the
// boundary as the statement is — the member is readonly, not connectionless.
try {
    $db->driver();
    echo "driver: not refused\n";
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    echo "driver: refused\n";
}

// `serverVersion()` is the other readonly member, and it is on the statement's
// side too — what it answers was kept on the connection, and the connection is
// what went back to the pool. A member answering a version after the release
// would be reading a string whose subject this request no longer holds.
try {
    $db->serverVersion();
    echo "serverVersion: not refused\n";
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    echo "serverVersion: refused\n";
}

// `isOpen()` is the exception, and deliberately: a member that asks whether
// the connection is gone cannot be one that throws because it is.
echo "isOpen: ", $db->isOpen() ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// And `close` is idempotent, which is the same rule read from the other end —
// it asks for a state that already holds.
$db->close();
echo "closed twice: ", $db->isOpen() ? "yes" : "no", "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# § 3: `db.connect` covers a SQLite block, whose path is configuration.
[capabilities.db]
connect = ["main"]

[db.main]
driver = "sqlite"
path = ":memory:"
--EXPECT--
created 0
query: refused
stream: refused
driver: refused
serverVersion: refused
isOpen: no
closed twice: no
